abstract: >-
  Unfunded empirical study comparing two periods of Indian government, the United
  Progressive Alliance (2004-2014) and the National Democratic Alliance (2014-2026),
  across growth, employment, distribution, fiscal federalism, statistical capacity and
  democratic quality. Constructs three indices - a Statistical Suppression Index, a
  Fiscal Centralisation Index and a Democratic Quality Index - computed for both eras by
  a single published script. On eight of nine directly comparable measures the later
  period performs worse; the exception is consumer price inflation, which is reported as
  an exception and argued out rather than claimed as a result. The author holds a
  Manmohan Singh Fellowship of the All India Professionals' Congress, a department of
  the Indian National Congress, and that interest is declared in the paper and on the
  site.
